NAME
     vmm - machines, containers and the hypervisor

SYNOPSIS
     vmm start [-n NAME] [-k KERNEL] [-f DISK|none] [-c NCPU] [-m MB]
               [-a BOOTARGS] [-i dhcp|IP/PREFIX[,GW[,DNS]]] [-w WEIGHT]
               [--cpu-max PCT] [-p HPORT[:GPORT][/udp]] [-M MB] [-H] [-s]
               [-4] [-d] [-r ADDR]
     vmm ls | ps [VM] | info VM | stats
     vmm attach VM | pause VM | resume VM | restart VM | kill VM|--all
     vmm balloon VM MB | ksm on|off | weight VM N | cpumax VM PCT
     vmm port VM [ls] | vmm port VM add|rm SPEC...
     vmm gw [ls] | vmm gw add NAME ADDR/PREFIX [pub IP] | vmm gw rm NAME
     vmm save VM FILE | vmm snapshot VM FILE | vmm restore FILE [-n NAME] [-d]
     vmm clone BASE NEW [-f DISK] [-d]
     vmm migrate VM HOST:PORT | vmm accept PORT [-n NAME] [-d]
     vmm run [-n NAME] --rootfs DIR [OPTIONS] [CMD ARG...]
     vmm stop NAME | vmm exec NAME CMD...

DESCRIPTION
     vmm is the front end to the hypervisor and to containers. A machine
     is a guest with its own memory, vcpus and emulated devices, run by
     this kernel's own G-stage hypervisor; a container is a group of
     processes of this kernel in namespaces of their own.

     `vmm start' makes a machine and attaches to its console; Ctrl-] goes
     back to the shell without stopping it. `vmm ls' says what there is,
     `vmm info' says what one of them is doing -- memory, exits by cause,
     its share of the processors -- and `vmm kill' ends it.

     A machine's memory can be given back to the host while it runs
     (`vmm balloon'), shared with other machines page by page when the
     pages are identical (`vmm ksm on'), and written down: `vmm save'
     with the machine stopped, `vmm snapshot' with it running, and `vmm
     restore' to bring it back. `vmm clone' makes a second machine that
     shares the first's pages until one of them writes. `vmm migrate'
     hands a machine to another host over a connection, memory first
     while it runs and the rest with it stopped, and `vmm accept' takes
     it at the other end.

     `vmm run' starts a container instead: a root of its own, its own
     process numbers, its own network, its own hostname, with limits on
     memory and processes, volumes from the host, and ports published to
     the machine's network. `vmm exec' enters one, `vmm stop' ends it.

OPTIONS
     -n NAME        what to call it
     -k KERNEL      the kernel to boot (default /kernel)
     -f DISK|none   the disk image, or no disk at all
     -c NCPU        how many vcpus
     -m MB          how much memory
     -M MB          the most it may have (the balloon's ceiling)
     -a BOOTARGS    the command line the guest is given
     -i SPEC        its address: dhcp, or IP/PREFIX[,GW[,DNS]]
     -p SPEC        publish a port: HPORT[:GPORT][/udp]
     -w WEIGHT      its share of the processors against everything else
     --cpu-max PCT  a ceiling on that share
     -H             no hypervisor extension inside (nested guests off)
     -s             single-step for debugging
     -4             Sv39 rather than Sv48 inside
     -d             detached: do not attach to the console
     -r ADDR        load the kernel at ADDR
     --rootfs DIR   vmm run: the container's root
     --mem PAGES    vmm run: how much memory it may have
     --pids N       vmm run: how many processes
     -v SPEC        vmm run: a volume, HOST:PATH[:ro]
     -e VAR=VALUE   vmm run: a variable in its environment
     --net host|none, --if NAME, --gw NAME, -i IP/PREFIX
                    vmm run: what network it is on

EXAMPLES
     vmm start -n web -m 128 -c 2 -i dhcp -p 8080:80
     vmm info web
     vmm snapshot web /snap.vst
     vmm migrate web 10.7.0.2:9000
     vmm run -n build --rootfs /containers/build --pids 64 /bin/sh

SEE ALSO
     cgrun(1), unshare(1), nsenter(1), ip(8), README-hyp.md
